[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Control Rig and Procedural Animation Pipelines in Unreal Engine"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Modern game development heavily relies on a hybrid approach combining traditional keyframed animation with runtime procedural generation. Unreal Engine's Control Rig framework provides a powerful toolkit to manipulate skeletal meshes dynamically at runtime, blending inverse kinematics (IK), physics simulations, and procedural adjustments seamlessly."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Prerequisites and Setup"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Enable the Control Rig and Full Body IK plugins in your Unreal Engine project settings."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Ensure your skeletal mesh has a correctly oriented skeleton hierarchy and proper bone naming conventions."},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating Your First Control Rig Asset"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"To begin, right-click inside your Content Browser, navigate to Animation, and select Control Rig. Assign your target Skeleton. Open the newly created Control Rig asset to access the Rig Graph and Viewport interface."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing Procedural Foot Placement"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"One of the most common applications of procedural animation is adapting a character's feet to uneven terrain using Line Traces. Inside the Control Rig execution stack, we can use nodes to query the ground height and offset the foot bones accordingly."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"// Conceptual Logic inside Control Rig Forward Graph
FVector FootLocation = GetBoneGlobalTransform("foot_l").GetLocation();
FHitResult HitResult;
LineTraceByChannel(FootLocation + FVector(0,0,50), FootLocation - FVector(0,0,100), HitResult);
if (HitResult.bBlockingHit) {
    SetBoneGlobalTransform("foot_l_ik_target", HitResult.ImpactPoint);
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Integrating with Animation Blueprints"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"To evaluate your Control Rig within a live animation graph, open your character's Animation Blueprint (AnimBP). Add the "Control Rig" node to your output pose graph, select your Control Rig class, and expose variables such as foot offset values or target locations to drive them from the event graph."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Performance Optimization Guidelines"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Limit the use of complex Full Body IK solvers on distant background characters."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Utilize LOD settings to disable or simplify Control Rig evaluation when characters are far from the camera."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Profile your execution graphs using the "stat Anim" and "stat ControlRig" console commands to pinpoint bottlenecks."}]
